Q2. Why is Python called Platform Independent?

Answer (Same style as Q1)

Python is called a platform-independent programming language because the byte code is platform 
independent.

When we write a Python program, the compiler first converts the source code (.py) into byte code (.pyc).

This byte code is not dependent on any operating system. It can be used on different operating systems 
like Windows, Linux, and macOS.

On every operating system, the Python Virtual Machine (PVM) converts the same byte code into machine 
code.

Because the same byte code can run on different operating systems with the help of the PVM, Python is 
called a platform-independent programming language.
